The '''2004 UEFA Super Cup''' was the 29th [[UEFA Super Cup]], an annual [[association football]] match contested by the winners of the previous season's [[UEFA Champions League]] and [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] competitions. The match was played at the [[Stade Louis II]] in [[Monaco]] on 27 August 2004 and contested by [[FC Porto|Porto]] of Portugal and [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] of Spain.

Porto qualified as winners of the [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League]], having defeated [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] of the French league 3–0 in the [[2004 UEFA Champions League Final|final]], and were appearing in the Super Cup for the third time, following victory in [[1987 UEFA Super Cup|1987]] and defeat in [[2003 UEFA Super Cup|2003]]. Meanwhile, Valencia were appearing as winners of the [[2003–04 UEFA Cup]], following victory over another French team, [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]]. It was their second Super Cup, having won in their only previous appearance in [[1980 UEFA Super Cup|1980]].

Watched by a crowd of 17,292, a goal from [[Rubén Baraja]] gave Valencia the lead after half an hour, before [[Marco Di Vaio]] doubled their lead midway through the second half. [[Ricardo Quaresma]] scored for Porto in the 78th minute, but they were unable to find an equaliser and Valencia won the match 2–1 to win their second Super Cup.

==Background==
[[File:Stade Louis II.JPG|thumb|The [[Stade Louis II]] was the venue for the [[UEFA Super Cup]] from 1998 to 2012.]]
[[FC Porto|Porto]] qualified for the Super Cup as the reigning [[UEFA Champions League]] winners. They had won the [[2003–04 UEFA Champions League]] beating [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] [[2004 UEFA Champions League Final|3–0]] to win the competition for the second time.<ref>{{cite web|title=Porto perform to perfection|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/019d-0e6ae5999141-82ce41064ec4-1000/|date=27 May 2004|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref> It would be Porto's third appearance in the competition was contesting the Super Cup for the third time. They won the competition in [[1987 UEFA Super Cup|1987]] beating [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]], while they lost in [[2003 UEFA Super Cup|2003]], to [[A.C. Milan|Milan]].<ref>{{cite web|title=2003: Shevchenko steals the show|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/history/2003/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref>

[[Valencia CF|Valencia]] had qualified for the competition as a result of winning the [[2003–04 UEFA Cup]]. They had beaten [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] 2–0 in the [[2004 UEFA Cup Final|final]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Valencia victorious in Gothenburg|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c50f17e47bc-7a8a35ada93d-1000/|last=Harte|first=Adrian|date=20 May 2004|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref> Valencia's only previous appearance in [[1980 UEFA Super Cup|1980]] resulted in victory, beating [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]].<ref>{{cite web|title=1980: Valencia profit from Felman's fortune|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/history/1980/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref> Porto and Valencia had previously met each other in two European competition matches. In the second round of the [[1989–90 UEFA Cup]], Porto eliminated the Spanish team with a 5–4 aggregate score, as result of a 3–1 home win and a 2–3 away loss.<ref>{{cite web|last=Ross|first=James M.|title=UEFA Cup 1989–90|url=https://www.rsssf.org/ec/ec198990.html|work=RSSSF.com|publisher=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation|access-date=1 May 2011|date=17 January 2008| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110514002039/http://www.rsssf.com/ec/ec198990.html| archive-date= 14 May 2011 | url-status= live}}</ref>

Soon after their European victories, Porto manager [[José Mourinho]] and Valencia manager [[Rafael Benítez]] parted with their clubs—Mourinho was hired by [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], while Benitez took [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]'s helm<ref name="coaches">{{cite web|title=Iberians eye Super Cup silverware|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f1085792-df34f475dab8-1000--iberians-eye-super-cup-silverware/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=1 May 2011|date=18 August 2004}}</ref>—and were therefore not able to lead their teams into the 2004 UEFA Super Cup. To replace them, Porto had hired [[Victor Fernández]], and Valencia brought back [[Claudio Ranieri]] for a second spell.<ref name="coaches"/> Besides the manager position, both clubs also went through significant squad changes. Porto sold Portuguese international playmaker [[Deco]] to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], and Portuguese defenders [[Ricardo Carvalho]] and [[Paulo Ferreira]] accompanied Mourinho to Chelsea.<ref name="Porto squad changes">{{cite web|title=Summer of surprises for Porto |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f113c804-29ec5abd4afd-1000--summer-of-surprises-for-porto/ |work=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=1 May 2011 |date=18 August 2004 }}</ref> Relevant signings included Portuguese internationals [[Ricardo Quaresma]], [[Hélder Postiga]] and [[Raul Meireles]]; Greek international and newly crowned [[UEFA Euro 2004]] champion [[Giourkas Seitaridis]]; and Brazilian international and [[2004 Copa América]] winner [[Diego (footballer, born 1985)|Diego]].<ref name="Porto squad changes"/> Ranieri brought to Valencia three Italian internationals: former [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] striker [[Marco Di Vaio]], and [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] players [[Bernardo Corradi]] and [[Stefano Fiore]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Ranieri back for unfinished business|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f1049791-155ce321ca85-1000--ranieri-back-for-unfinished-business/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=1 May 2011|date=18 August 2004}}</ref>

Coming into the match after their previous week loss at the [[2004 Supercopa de España|Supercopa de España]], Valencia captain [[David Albelda]] admitted the team was "not at 100 per cent" and was still "hurt by the defeat", but this setback should help them "go into the match fully concentrated and go all out to win".<ref>{{cite web|last=Harte|first=Simon|title=Captain Albelda's call to arms|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f1db10dd-33beefc948a5-1000--captain-albelda-s-call-to-arms/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=7 May 2011|date=26 August 2004}}</ref> On the other hand, Porto were coming from another [[2004 Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira|Supertaça Cândido de Oliveira]] victory. The team's midfielder and captain [[Costinha]] assured that Valencia would be "an even tougher match" as it is "a very experienced team with good players". Remembering the Super Cup defeat against Milan in the previous year, Costinha showed no doubts: "... this time things are going to be different. I'm confident we can win".<ref>{{cite web|last=Tavares|first=Nuno|title=Costinha shows desire|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f1d69ced-78da125ce9fa-1000--costinha-shows-desire/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=8 May 2011|date=26 August 2004}}</ref>

Nonetheless, Porto had two key players ruled out due to injuries. Brazilian striker [[Derlei]] suffered a right knee ligament lesion during a friendly match on 15 August with city rivals [[Boavista F.C.|Boavista]],<ref>{{cite web|title=Derlei doubtful for Porto|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f115d6e0-362214dee5ed-1000--derlei-doubtful-for-porto/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=1 May 2011|date=17 August 2004}}</ref> whereas Diego suffered a thigh injury during the Portuguese Super Cup match.<ref>{{cite web|title=Diego to miss Monaco showpiece|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50f108559f-7384be64d3f5-1000--diego-to-miss-monaco-showpiece/|work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|access-date=1 May 2011|date=23 August 2004}}</ref>

